---
// Enemy State Machines — the full pattern reference (Unit 4 + Unit 8 boss edition).
import Base from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const crumbs = `<nav class="crumbs"><a href="../index.html">Docs</a> &gt; <a href="../unit-04/index.html">Unit 4</a> &gt; <span>Enemy State Machines</span></nav>`;

const body = `<h1>Enemy State Machines</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Every enemy you build this year — the patrolling guard, the turret
    that shoots, the boss with three phases — is the same idea: a thing
    with a small set of behaviors, one of which is active, and the
    conditions that trade between them. This is the pattern reference,
    from the guard in Unit 4 to the boss in Unit 8.
</p>

<h2>What a state machine IS (in AP words)</h2>
<p>
    A state machine is <strong>state stored in fields</strong>
    (<a href="../../lessons/1.3.html">1.3</a>), <strong>selection
    choosing between behaviors</strong> (AAP-3), and <strong>Update
    providing the iteration</strong> that asks the conditions over and
    over. The exam's definition of an algorithm — sequencing,
    selection, iteration — is the whole shape of an enemy controller:
</p>
<pre class="lang-cs"><code>private void Update()          // ITERATION - runs ~60 times a second
{
    // SELECTION  - which behaviour is active right now?
    // SEQUENCING - run exactly one of them
}</code></pre>

<h2>The guard's machine (Unit 4)</h2>
<p>
    The enemy controller you build in Unit 4 is a machine whose states are
    mostly <em>implicit</em> — they exist as conditions, not as a named enum.
    That is a legitimate design, and worth understanding before you decide
    whether to upgrade it:
</p>
<table>
    <tr><th>State</th><th>What makes it active</th><th>Transition out</th></tr>
    <tr><td>PATROL</td><td>nothing else is true — it is the default behaviour</td><td>→ CHASE once the player is inside the aggro radius</td></tr>
    <tr><td>CHASE</td><td>the player is within range, so the chase condition matches</td><td>→ PATROL when the player leaves that radius</td></tr>
    <tr><td>ATTACK</td><td>a contact event, not a move state — it fires <em>during</em> patrol or chase</td><td>→ back to whatever movement state is still true, after a cooldown</td></tr>
</table>
<p>
    The elegant part: there is no "return to patrol" line anywhere.
    The chase sits inside an if; when the if stops matching, the enemy
    simply falls back to its default behavior. The patrol was never
    switched OFF — it was outvoted. Drawn on paper
    (<a href="../../lessons/4.5.html">4.5</a>), the machine is four
    boxes and three arrows; coded, it is three ifs and a cooldown.
</p>
<p>
    Notice something important about ATTACK: it is not a movement state at all.
    It is triggered by physics, so it can happen while the enemy is patrolling
    <em>or</em> chasing. That is the single most common design mistake in a
    first state machine — assuming every state must be mutually exclusive with
    every other.
</p>

<h2>The turret, minimized</h2>
<p>
    Even the turret is a two-state machine: SHOOTING (in range AND off
    cooldown) and WAITING — and the cooldown pattern
    (<a href="../../lessons/3.3.html">3.3</a>) is what turns "in
    range" into a rhythm. A timer plus a comparison IS a state machine
    with a clock for a brain. This is why the cooldown pattern reappears
    everywhere: attack rates, contact damage, jump mercies
    (<a href="../../lessons/6.3.html">6.3</a>), turn gates
    (<a href="../../lessons/10.2d.html">10.2d</a>).
</p>

<h2>Making states explicit (the enum upgrade)</h2>
<p>
    When a machine grows past "a couple of ifs," named states earn their
    ceremony. The upgrade has four parts, and you can write them yourself
    from the descriptions alone:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>An <strong>enum</strong> declaring the states as named values, so the compiler can catch a typo that a string could not</li>
    <li>One <strong>field</strong> holding the currently active state</li>
    <li>A <strong>switch on that field</strong> inside the update loop, with one case per state</li>
    <li>Each case <strong>assigning the next state</strong> when its condition is met — that assignment <em>is</em> the transition</li>
</ul>
<p>
    Two rules make explicit machines trustworthy:
    <strong>one state active at a time</strong> (if two can be active,
    you did not need a state machine), and <strong>every transition
    has a condition you can read aloud</strong> ("recovering ends when
    the interval has elapsed"). A machine whose transitions you cannot
    narrate is a pile of ifs wearing a costume.
</p>

<h2>Do not assume the animator agrees with you</h2>
<p>
    A state machine in code and a state machine in the Animator are two
    different machines that happen to share a vocabulary, and they do not
    have to use the same conventions. This project proves it. The Player's
    controller and the enemy's controller were built by different hands and
    they disagree on almost everything:
</p>
<table>
    <tr><th></th><th>Player</th><th>Enemy</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Direction parameter</td><td><code>WalkDir</code>: 0 = up, 1 = sideways, 2 = down</td><td><code>Direction</code>: 0 = right, 1 = up, 2 = left, 3 = down</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Idle states?</td><td>Yes — idle <em>and</em> walk states</td><td>No — walk states only</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Left and right</td><td>One sideways clip, mirrored with <code>flipX</code></td><td>Separate left and right clips, no <code>flipX</code> at all</td></tr>
</table>
<p>
    The lesson: read a state machine before you drive it. Two controllers that
    both have a direction parameter can still mean completely different
    things by <code>0</code>. When you drive someone else's machine — including
    one you wrote last month — open the Animator window and check its
    parameters rather than assuming they match the last one you met.
</p>

<h2>The boss's phase layer (Unit 8)</h2>
<p>
    The boss stacks a second machine ON TOP of the behavior one:
    <strong>phases</strong> — a one-way ladder driven by health
    thresholds (<a href="../../lessons/8.2.html">8.2</a>). Two design
    facts make phases work:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>Phase changes fire DOWNWARD ONLY (currentPhase guards in the
        threshold check), so a phase entrance never re-runs</li>
    <li>Phase changes swap NUMBERS via a phase table
        (<a href="../../lessons/8.4.html">8.4</a>) — cooldowns,
        speeds, attack sets — so the player SEES difficulty change
        without a single new mechanic being taught</li>
</ul>
<p>
    A phase is "the same machine with different constants" — which is
    also the exam's cleanest example of managing complexity: one
    table, one switch statement, six difficulty knobs.
</p>

<h2>Debugging a state machine</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Instrument the trade:</strong> Debug.Log the state
        name on every transition, watch the console flicker at
        boundaries — a machine that flickers CHASE/PATROL at the range
        edge is telling you the condition has no hysteresis (a bigger
        chase-in radius than chase-out radius fixes it; that gap is
        what <a href="../../lessons/6.3.html">6.3</a> calls mercy,
        pointed at AI instead of the player)</li>
    <li><strong>Reproduce before you theorize:</strong> stand where
        the tester stood, in the state they were in. Most "AI bugs"
        are boundary bugs at the transition line.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the guards:</strong> a transition that fires
        every frame is an unguarded if; a transition that never fires
        is a wrong comparison or a wrong tag. Both are one Debug.Log
        away.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Where you meet machines this year</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Patrol / chase / attack / return:
        <a href="../../lessons/4.5.html">4.5</a> (drawn),
        <a href="../../lessons/4.1.html">4.1</a>–<a href="../../lessons/4.4.html">4.4</a> (built)</li>
    <li>Cooldowns as clock-machines:
        <a href="../../lessons/3.3.html">3.3</a>,
        <a href="../../lessons/6.3.html">6.3</a></li>
    <li>Climb state (a movement state machine):
        <a href="../../lessons/7.3.html">7.3</a></li>
    <li>Boss phases:
        <a href="../../lessons/8.2.html">8.2</a> (thresholds),
        <a href="../../lessons/8.4.html">8.4</a> (attack tables)</li>
    <li>Turn gates in the card jam:
        <a href="../../lessons/10.2d.html">10.2d</a></li>
</ul>

<div class="unit-nav">
    <span><a href="../unit-04/index.html">← Unit 4</a></span>
    <span><a href="../index.html">All docs</a></span>
    <span><a href="../unit-08/index.html">Unit 8: Boss Fight →</a></span>
</div>`;
